course: biology
lesson: 240
title: Wolves and teamwork: family packs, the alpha myth and hunting together
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A wild wolf pack is not a gang ruled by a fighting boss: it is a family, a mother and father wolf with their young from the last few years.
explain: The «alpha wolf» idea came from unrelated wolves kept together in zoos, who fought; in the wild the leaders are simply the parents.
explain: Picture a family walking through deep snow: the parents go first and break the path, and the children step in their footprints, which is just what a wolf pack does.
explain: Wolves, lionesses, African wild dogs and even geese in a V show that working as a team can beat being the strongest on your own.
fact: Most wolf packs are a family: a mother, a father and their young of different years, and their land can cover hundreds of square kilometres.
fact: Leading wolves break a path through snow and the others walk in their tracks to save energy.
fact: The alpha idea came from zoo wolves that were strangers to each other; David Mech, who made the word famous, spent years asking people to stop using it.
fact: Real wolves rarely attack people and usually avoid them, and bringing wolves back to Yellowstone in 1995 let riverbank trees grow back as elk, a kind of big deer, grazed less there.
fact: Wolves howl to keep the pack in touch and tell other packs to stay away; they howl at any time, more at dusk and at night, but not at the moon; they just point their heads up so the sound carries.
fact: Wolves test a herd to find a weak, young, old or sick animal, most hunts fail, and a wolf can eat about 9 kilograms of meat at once.
fact: Wolf puppies learn by play; a big wolf often lets a small one win, and a play bow with the bottom up means «this is play», just as in dogs.
fact: Lionesses hunt together, some driving prey towards others hiding in ambush, so they can catch animals as strong as buffalo.
fact: African wild dogs, also called painted wolves, seem to vote by sneezing on whether to go hunting, and they share food with pups and the sick.
fact: Geese fly in a V to save energy, each riding the upward swirl of air from the bird in front, and they take turns at the front.
fact: Dogs came from wolves tamed more than 15,000 years ago, and even a Chihuahua has 42 teeth like a wolf.
fact: Spotted hyenas hunt most of their own food in teams, their «laugh» is a call of excitement or stress, and their clans are led by females, who are bigger than the males.
exercise: What is a wild wolf pack usually made of? | A family: mother, father and their young | Strangers who fight each other for the top place | Only male wolves | Wolves of many different packs
exercise: Why do wolves walk in a line through deep snow? | The leaders break the path and the others save energy | They are scared of each other | They are counting the trees as they go past | They cannot see each other when they walk side by side
exercise: Where did the «alpha wolf» idea come from? | Unrelated wolves kept together in zoos who fought | Watching wild wolf families out in the forest for many years | Old fairy tales only | A dog training book
exercise: Who are the real leaders of a wild pack? | The mother and father wolves | The strongest young male in the pack | The smallest puppy, protected by all | A different wolf each day, taking turns
exercise: What did David Mech ask people to do? | Stop using the word «alpha» for wild wolves | Hunt more wolves | Keep wolves as pets at home | Teach wolves to howl at the moon every night
exercise: How do wild wolves usually behave towards people? | They rarely attack and usually avoid them | They hunt people for food | They follow people about for fun, like dogs | They blow houses down
exercise: What happened in Yellowstone after wolves came back in 1995? | Elk grazed riverbanks less and trees grew back | All the elk disappeared | The park was closed | The trees along the rivers were eaten by the wolves
exercise: Why do wolves howl? | To keep in touch and warn other packs away | Because they see the moon | Because they are hungry for cheese | To make the moon rise
exercise: Which animal in a herd do wolves try to catch? | A weak, young, old or sick one | The strongest one | Always the leader of the herd | Any one at random
exercise: How much meat can a wolf eat at once? | About 9 kilograms | About 9 grams | About 90 kilos | Only a few bites
exercise: What does a play bow, bottom up, mean? | This is play, not a fight | I am about to attack you now | I am very tired and want to sleep | Go away and leave me alone
exercise: How do lionesses hunt together? | Some drive prey towards others hiding in ambush | Each one hunts alone, far away from all the others | The male lion does all of the hunting for the whole pride | They just wait for old or sick prey to die by itself
exercise: How do African wild dogs seem to decide to go hunting? | By sneezing, like a vote | By howling at the moon together | By fighting to see who goes first | By following the sun as it rises
exercise: Why do geese fly in a V shape? | Each bird rides the upward swirl of air from the one in front | To look like a letter in the sky | To keep the leader warm | To scare away the eagles and hawks that hunt them
exercise: How long ago did wolves start to become dogs? | More than 15,000 years ago | About 100 years ago, quite recently | About 1,000 years ago, in castle times | About 1 million years ago, or more
exercise: Who leads a clan of spotted hyenas? | Females, who are bigger than the males | The oldest and the biggest of the male hyenas | The lions | Nobody at all
test: «Big Bad Wolf blew the house down, wolves r evil» What is the real picture? | Real wolves rarely attack people and usually avoid them | Wolves often blow houses down | Wolves hunt children for fun, just like in the fairy tales | Wolves live in houses
test: A friend says: «wolves howl at the full moon, everyone knows that». What is true? | They howl at any time, more at dusk and night, heads up so the sound carries | They howl only at the full moon, because its bright light hurts their eyes so much | They howl only at the Sun | They never howl
test: A cartoon shows laughing hyenas that only steal from lions. What is more accurate? | Hyenas hunt most of their own food, and their «laugh» is excitement or stress | Hyenas never hunt | Hyenas really laugh at jokes | Hyenas are a kind of dog that only eats what lions leave behind on the plain
test: «my coach says he's the alpha and we're the pack lol» What do real wolves teach? | Wild packs are led by parents, not by a fighting boss | The strongest wolf always beats everyone else in the pack and rules | Packs have no leaders at all | Alphas eat the pups
test: What would happen if a lone wolf tried to chase a healthy, fast elk? | It would probably fail, as most hunts do | It would always catch it | The elk would chase the wolf all the way home | The elk would give up at once
test: Watching geese, you see the front bird drop back and another take its place. Why? | The front is hardest, so they take turns | The leader got lost | They are racing | The front bird fell asleep in the air and slipped back
test: «my dog's a Chihuahua, nothing like a wolf» What might surprise him? | His Chihuahua has the same 42 teeth and came from wolves | Chihuahuas came from cats | Chihuahuas have only 10 teeth | Chihuahuas came from foxes, not from wolves like other dogs
test: A big wolf lets a little one pin it down during play. Why? | So play goes on and the small one learns | The big wolf is weak | It is really hurt | It wants to be the pup again and be fed by the others
test: Count them: a pack of 7 wolves trots through snow. How many legs, ears and tails? | 28 legs, 14 ears and 7 tails | 14 legs, 14 ears and 7 tails | 28 legs, 7 ears and 7 tails | 28 legs, 14 ears and 14 tails
test: Count them: 5 lionesses and 4 wild dogs share a plain. How many legs in all? | 36 legs | 18 legs | 32 legs | 40 legs
test: «wild dogs sneezing before a hunt, they got colds?» What is going on? | The sneezes seem to work like a vote to set off | They are all ill | They smelled pepper | They are scaring away the flies that buzz round their noses
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the composition of a wolf pack in the wild? | It ordinarily consists of a breeding pair and their offspring of several years | It consists of unrelated adults contending for dominance | It consists exclusively of males | It is reconstituted at random every season, its members being drawn from many packs
exam: Identify the origin of the notion of the «alpha wolf». | Observations of unrelated wolves confined together in captivity | Prolonged observation of wild wolf families in their natural habitat | The scientific study of domestic dogs alone | The behaviour of wolves upon the full moon
exam: Ascertain the principal advantage obtained by geese flying in a V formation. | A reduction in the energy expended, each bird being lifted by the air from the bird in front | Protection of the leading bird from cold | Improved observation of the ground | Intimidation of birds of prey, the formation resembling a single bird of very great size
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the consequences of the reintroduction of wolves into Yellowstone in 1995. | The grazing of riverbanks by elk diminished and trees recovered | The elk population was entirely extinguished | The park was closed to visitors | The wolves consumed the trees of the riverbanks in great quantity
